cteria. Appl Environ Microbiol. 2000;66(3):1126&#x2013;1132.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC91952</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10698781</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pancost RD, Hopmans EC, Sinninghe Damst&#xe9; JS. Archaeal lipids in Mediterranean cold seeps: Molecular proxies for anaerobic methane oxidation. Geochim Cosmochim Acta. 2001;65:1611&#x2013;1627.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pease TK, van Vleet ES, Barre JS, Dickins HD. Simulated degradation of glyceril ethers by hydrous and flach pyrolysis. Org Geochem. 1998;29:979&#x2013;988.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bolle M-P, et al. The Paleocene-Eocene transition in the marginal northeastern Tethys (Kazakstan and Uzbekistan) Int J Earth Sci. 2000;89:390&#x2013;414.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Moore TS, Murray RW, Kurtz AC, Schrag DP. Anaerobic methane oxidation and the formation of dolomite. Earth Planet Sci Lett. 2004;229:141&#x2013;154.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Malone MJ, Claypool G, Martin JB, Dickens GR. Variable methane fluxes in shallow marine systems over geologic time: The composition and origin of pore waters and authigenic carbonates on the New Jersey shelf. Mar Geol. 2002;189:175&#x2013;196.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Arndt S, Brumsack H-J, Wirtz KW. Cretaceous black shales as active bioreactors: A biogeochemical model for the deep biosphere encountered during ODP Leg 207 (Demerara Rise) Geochim Cosmochim Acta. 2006;70:408&#x2013;425.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Boudreau BP, Ruddick BR. On a reactive continuum representation of organic matter diagenesis. Am J Sci. 1991;291:507&#x2013;538.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wefer G, Heinze SP-M, Suess E (1990) Stratigraphy and sedimentation rates from oxygen isotope composition, organic carbon content, and grain-size distribution at the Peru Upwelling Region: Holes 680B and 686B. Proceedings of the Ocean Drilling Program, eds Suess E, et al. (Ocean Drilling Program, College Station, TX), Vol 112, pp 355&#x2013;367.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tissot BP, Welte DH. Petroleum Formation and Occurrence. New York: Springer; 1984.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Suess E, et al., eds (1988) Proceedings of the Ocean Drilling Program (Ocean Drilling Program, College Station, TX), Vol 112.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Schulz HD, Zabel M. In: Quantification of early diagenesis: Dissolved constituents in marine pore water. Marine Geochemistry. Schulz HD, Zabel M, editors. Berlin: Springer; 2006. pp. 271&#x2013;310.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Boudreau BP. Diagenetic Models and Their Implementation: Modelling Transport and Reactions in Aquatic Sediments. Berlin: Springer; 1997.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yamamoto S, Alcauskas JB, Crozler TE. Solubility of methane in distilled water and seawater. J Chem Eng Data. 1976;21:78&#x2013;80.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wiesenburg DA, Guinasso NL., Jr Equilibrium solubilities of methane, carbon monoxide, and hydrogen in water and seawater. J Chem Eng Data. 1979;24:356&#x2013;360.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tarpgaard IH, R&#xf8;y H, J&#xf8;rgensen BB. Concurrent low- and high-affinity sulfate reduction kinetics in marine sediment. Geochim Cosmochim Acta. 2011;75:2997&#x2013;3010.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nauhaus K, Boetius A, Kr&#xfc;ger M, Widdel F. In vitro demonstration of anaerobic oxidation of methane coupled to sulfate reduction in sediment from marine gas hydrate area. Environ Microbiol. 1995;4:298&#x2013;305.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12080959</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Knab NJ, Dale AW, Lettmann K, Fossing H, J&#xf8;rgensen BB. Thermodynamic and kinetic control on anaerobic oxidation of methane in marine sediments. Geochim Cosmochim Acta. 2008;72:3746&#x2013;3757.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sukop MC, Thorne DT., Jr . Lattice Boltzmann Modeling: An Introduction for Geoscientists and Engineers. Heidelberg: Springer; 2006.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">24136469</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2014</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2014</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>01</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1432-0614</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>98</Volume><Issue>7</Issue><PubDate><Year>2014</Year><Month>Apr</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Applied microbiology and biotechnology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Appl Microbiol Biotechnol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Microbial community and function of enrichment cultures with methane and toluene.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>3121</StartPage><EndPage>3131</EndPage><MedlinePgn>3121-31</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00253-013-5297-8</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The interaction effect of co-existence of toluene and CH4 on community and activity of methanotrophs and toluene-degrading bacteria was characterized in three consortia enriched with CH4 and toluene (MT), toluene (T), and CH4 (M), respectively, in this study. The CH4 oxidation activity in the enrichment culture of MT was significantly lower than that of M at the end of the experiment (P&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.001). The toluene degradation rate could be enhanced by continuous addition of CH4 and toluene in the initial days, but it was inhibited in the later days. Phylogenetic analysis of 16S rRNA genes showed that Proteobacteria and Bacteroidetes were dominant in the three enriched consortia, but the community of methanotrophs and toluene-degrading bacteria was significantly affected by the co-existence of CH4 and toluene. Both Methylosinus (91.8 %) and Methylocystis (8.2 %) were detected in the enrichment culture of MT, while only Methylocystis species were detected in M. The toluene-degrading bacteria including Burkholderia, Flavobacteria, Microbacterium, and Azoarcus were all detected in the enrichment culture of T. However, only Azoarcus was found in the enrichment culture of MT. Significantly higher contents of extracellular polymeric substances polysaccharose and protein in the enrichment culture of MT than that of T and M suggested that a higher environmental stress occurred in the enrichment culture of MT.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Su</LastName><ForeName>Yao</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310058, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xia</LastName><ForeName>Fang-Fang</ForeName><Initials>FF</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tian</LastName><ForeName>Bao-Hu</ForeName><Initials>BH</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Wei</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>He</LastName><ForeName>Ruo</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><DataBankList CompleteYN="Y"><DataBank><DataBankName>GENBANK</DataBankName><AccessionNumberList><AccessionNumber>KC352356</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352357</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352358</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352359</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352360</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352361</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352362</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352363</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352364</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352365</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352366</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>KC352367</AccessionNumber><Accessio