he consumption of atmospheric methane in two forest soils. Environ. Microbiol. 7:1150&#x2013;1161</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16011752</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dumont MG, Pommerenke B, Casper P, Conrad R. 2011. DNA-, rRNA- and mRNA-based stable isotope probing of aerobic methanotrophs in lake sediment. Environ. Microbiol. 13:1153&#x2013;1167</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21261798</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Gilbert B, Frenzel P. 1995. Methanotrophic bacteria in the rhizosphere of rice microcosms and their effect on porewater methane concentration and methane emission. Biol. Fertil. Soils 20:93&#x2013;100</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bodelier PLE, Roslev P, Henckel T, Frenzel P. 2000. Stimulation by ammonium-based fertilizers of methane oxidation in soil around rice roots. Nature 403:421&#x2013;424</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10667792</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mohanty SR, Bodelier PLE, Floris V, Conrad R. 2006. Differential effects of nitrogenous fertilizers on methane-consuming microbes in rice field and forest soils. Appl. Environ. Microbiol. 72:1346&#x2013;1354</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1392950</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16461686</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>
